Cortex-A53: set CPUECTLR_EL1.SMPEN so exclusive accesses can succeed.

The kernel hangs, with no fault and no reset, inside lck_mtx_init_ext() at the
end of lck_mod_init() -- before any console exists, so the only symptom is a
screen that stops updating. lck_mod_init() is pure software, which is what
makes it confusing: a queue, a bzero, a strncpy, two attribute defaults and a
mutex initialiser, none of which should care what board they run on.

What they do contain is the first atomic read-modify-write in the kernel's
life. lck_mtx_init_ext() ends with

    lck_grp_reference(grp);
    lck_grp_lckcnt_incr(grp, LCK_TYPE_MTX);

and both reduce to hw_atomic_add, which on arm64 is an ldxr/stxr pair that
retries until the store-exclusive reports success.

On a Cortex-A53 that store never succeeds while CPUECTLR_EL1.SMPEN is clear.
The TRM states that the core does not support the coherency or exclusive
monitor features with the bit at 0, and the kernel maps its own memory with
ARM_TTE_BOOT_BLOCK, which carries SH_OUTER_MEMORY -- Normal, shareable. An
exclusive access to shareable memory from a core outside the coherency domain
fails every time, so the retry loop never terminates. Nothing faults, which is
exactly why this is hard to see.

Apple's cores have no such register, so xnu never writes it, and on this board
nothing else does either.

The TRM also requires the bit to be set before the caches and the MMU are
enabled, and before any cache or TLB maintenance is performed. That rules out
common_start, which invalidates the bootstrap tables and issues tlbi, so this
goes in start_first_cpu immediately after the boot-args pointer has been saved
to x20 -- the earliest point where a scratch register is free. x6 is used
because start.s only ever touches it inside create_bootstrap_mapping, much
later; x0 still holds boot_args here and is left alone.

Writing the bit is idempotent, so this is harmless on firmware that has
already set it. It is guarded on BCM2837 because CPUECTLR_EL1 does not exist
on the Apple targets this file is otherwise built for.

--- a/osfmk/arm64/start.s
+++ b/osfmk/arm64/start.s
@@ -455,6 +455,34 @@
 	mov		x20, x0
 	mov		x21, #0
 
+#ifdef BCM2837
+	/*
+	 * Cortex-A53: join the coherency domain before anything depends on it.
+	 *
+	 * CPUECTLR_EL1.SMPEN (S3_1_C15_C2_1, bit 6) enables coherency and, with
+	 * it, the exclusive monitor. The TRM is explicit that the core does not
+	 * support either while the bit is clear, and that it has to be set
+	 * before the caches and the MMU are enabled or any cache or TLB
+	 * maintenance is performed -- which is why it is done here and not in
+	 * common_start.
+	 *
+	 * With SMPEN clear, ldxr/stxr against Normal shareable memory never
+	 * succeeds. Nothing faults: the first atomic read-modify-write in the
+	 * kernel's life simply retries forever. That is lck_mtx_init_ext()
+	 * incrementing a lock group's refcount at the end of lck_mod_init(),
+	 * long before any console exists, so the symptom is a frozen screen
+	 * with nothing to say why.
+	 *
+	 * Apple cores have no such register and xnu therefore never touches it.
+	 * Writing it is idempotent, so this is safe on firmware that already
+	 * set the bit.
+	 */
+	mrs		x6, S3_1_C15_C2_1				// CPUECTLR_EL1
+	orr		x6, x6, #(1 << 6)				// SMPEN
+	msr		S3_1_C15_C2_1, x6
+	isb		sy
+#endif /* BCM2837 */
+
 	// Set low reset vector before attempting any loads
 	adrp	x0, EXT(LowExceptionVectorBase)@page
 	add		x0, x0, EXT(LowExceptionVectorBase)@pageoff
